*** Resetting replication at the very beginning of the test.

*** Generating a configuration file in JSON format for the Binlog
*** Server utility.

*** Determining binlog file directory from the server.

*** Creating a temporary directory <BINSRV_STORAGE_PATH> for storing
*** binlog files downloaded via the Binlog Server utility.

*** Starting Binlog Server Utility in background in pull mode.

*** Waiting for the replication source listener port ready to accept
*** connections.

*** No public key: client picks the same plugin the server advertises
*** (caching_sha2_password), the Binlog Server requests full
*** authentication, but the client has neither a secure connection nor
*** the server's RSA public key to encrypt the password, so it must
*** give up and mysql must return non-zero exit code.

*** Public key: same as above, but the client is given the server's RSA
*** public key via --server-public-key-path, so it sends the password
*** (XOR-ed with the scramble) encrypted with this key. A zero exit
*** code from mysql confirms the session got as far as running the
*** probe query.

*** Public key, wrong password: the Binlog Server successfully decrypts
*** the password, but it does not match the configured one, so access
*** must be denied and mysql must return non-zero exit code.

*** Auth method switch + public key: client forces mysql_native_password
*** in its handshake so the Binlog Server has to send an
*** AuthMethodSwitch, after which the client switches to
*** caching_sha2_password and goes through the full authentication with
*** the server's RSA public key. If the switch encoding, the follow-up
*** parse, or the full authentication path is broken, mysql returns
*** non-zero and MTR fails the --exec. The log grep that follows the
*** shutdown is what actually proves the switch happened - this line
*** only proves the session survived it.

*** Requesting public key: client asks the Binlog Server for its RSA
*** public key (--get-server-public-key) instead of using a local copy.
*** The Binlog Server extracts the public component from its RSA
*** private key and sends it in PEM format, after which the client
*** encrypts the password with this key. A zero exit code from mysql
*** confirms the session got as far as running the probe query.

*** Requesting public key, wrong password: the client receives the
*** public key and sends the encrypted password, the Binlog Server
*** successfully decrypts it, but it does not match the configured one,
*** so access must be denied and mysql must return non-zero exit code.

*** Auth method switch + requesting public key: client forces
*** mysql_native_password in its handshake, then switches to
*** caching_sha2_password and goes through the full authentication
*** requesting the server's RSA public key (this shifts the sequence
*** numbers of all subsequent packets by 2).

*** Sending SIGTERM signal to the Binlog Server Utility and waiting for
*** the process to terminate

*** Confirming the switch actually happened by grepping the Binlog
*** Server stdout log (now complete and flushed) for the marker
*** emitted after generate_encoded_auth_method_switch() writes its
*** frame.
include/wait_for_pattern_in_file.inc [sent server auth method switch]

*** Confirming the full authentication was requested (marker emitted
*** after generate_encoded_full_auth() writes its frame).
include/wait_for_pattern_in_file.inc [sent server full auth required]

*** Confirming the full authentication actually succeeded at least
*** once (for the sessions with the correct password and public key).
include/wait_for_pattern_in_file.inc [client authentication succeeded]

*** Confirming the public key was actually sent to the client (marker
*** emitted after generate_encoded_public_key() writes its frame).
include/wait_for_pattern_in_file.inc [sent server RSA public key]

*** Removing the Binlog Server utility storage directory.

*** Removing the Binlog Server utility log file.

*** Removing the Binlog Server utility configuration file.
